<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-12-1">
  <title>Resources for 12/1</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the statement of the <em>Intermediate Value Theorem</em>. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain how infima and/or suprema are used to prove the Intermediate Value Theorem, and thus how this theorem is firmly connected to the Completeness Axiom.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use the Intermediate Value Theorem to prove theorems about continuous functions.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Have a concrete understanding of the \emph{derivative} (e.g. as the slope of the function, the slope of the tangent line, the limit of the slopes of secant lines,  or as a rate of change).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Make connections between your concrete understandings of the derivative and the formal definition of the derivative.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Compute the derivatives of functions using the definition of the derivative.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove theorems about derivatives using the definition of the derivative.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Resist the self-destructive impulse to use the definition of \emph{limit} when finding the derivative of your function, unless your mean old professor instructs you to do so.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-ivt-2">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Intermediate Value Theorem</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video on the <em>Intermediate Value Theorem</em>, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Intermediate Value Theorem</title>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>f: [a,b] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> be a continuous function, and let <m>c \in \mathbb{R}</m> with <m>f(a) &lt; c &lt; f(b)</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Draw a picture of this situation, along with some possible graphs for <m>f</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Let <m>t</m> be the first value of <m>x</m> where <m>f(x)=c</m> and <m>s</m> be the last value of <m>x</m> where <m>f(x)=c</m>.  What is true about <m>f</m> on <m>[a,t)</m>?  What about on <m>(s,b]</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Define <m>S = \{ x \in [a,b] : f(x) \leq c \}</m> and <m>T = \{ x \in [a,b] : f(x) \geq c \}</m>. What is <m>\sup(S)</m> in your pictures?  What is <m>\inf(T)</m> in your pictures?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>f</m>, <m>S</m>, and <m>T</m> be as above.  Define <m>s = \sup(S)</m>, and <m>t= \inf(T)</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that <m>S</m> and <m>T</m> are bounded.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Prove that there are sequences <m>\{ s_n \}</m> and <m>\{ t_n \}</m> with each <m>s_n \in S</m> and each <m>t_n \in T</m> such that <m>s_n \rightarrow s</m> and <m>t_n \rightarrow t</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  To what do <m>\{ f(s_n) \}</m> and <m>\{ f(t_n) \}</m> converge?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is the largest possible value of <m>f(s)</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is the smallest possible value of <m>f(t)</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What is true about all elements of <m>(s,b]</m>?  What about <m>[a,t)</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Use what you found above to determine <m>f(s)</m> and <m>f(t)</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-differentiation">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for the Derivative</title>
    <p>
      Below are links to a video on the <em>derivative of a function</em>, which is available both on Google Drive and YouTube. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Derivative</title>
      <p>
        This section will not be assessed on a quiz or the final exam, but I wanted to let you know how our work contributes to differential calculus.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute <m>f'(a)</m> from the limit definition.
            <ol>
              <li>
                  <p>
                    <m>f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m>, <m>f(x) = x^2+2x</m>
                  </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>f: (0,\infty) \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m>, <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{x}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Suppose that <m>f'(a)</m> exists.  Prove that <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>x=a</m>.  Hint:  Prove that <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \rightarrow a} [f(x) - f(a)] = 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Suppose we know <m>f'(a)</m> and <m>g'(a)</m>.  Find <m>h'(a)</m>, where
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>h(x) = k f(x)</m>, <m>k</m> a constant.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>h(x) = f(x)+g(x)</m>
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>h(x) = \frac{1}{g(x)}</m>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>h(x) = f(x)g(x)</m>.  Suppose you know <m>f'(a)</m> and <m>g'(a)</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  What are the dimensions of a rectangle with area <m>h(x)</m>?  What about <m>h(a)</m>?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Carefully draw one of the above rectangles inside the other and find a formula for <m>h(x)-h(a)</m>.  Verify the formula algebraically.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Use your result in (b) to find <m>h'(a)</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>h(x) = \frac{f(x)}{g(x)}</m>, and suppose you know <m>f'(a)</m> and <m>g'(a)</m>.  Compute <m>h'(a)</m>.  Hint: Use #3(c) and #4.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

